// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row localvec_for_ref_names_stdlib_as_slice (tier 4, `diag`).
// THE RIGHT VERDICT, THROUGH THE WRONG TYPE, PRINTED AS TWO IDENTICAL STRINGS.
//
// ⚠ RE-MEASURED AND RE-POINTED 2026-09-10d. The door this row was FILED against
// is gone and the row is NOT: `sema_stmt.cpp`'s `&Vec<T>` -> `as_slice()`
// desugar was qualified with `is_stdlib_vec` in that round and no longer fires
// for a package-local `Vec`. The scrutinee then falls through to the
// IntoIterator desugar three blocks below, which resolves
// `find_func_candidates(base + "__into_iter")` where `base` is the BARE struct
// name — so it finds the stdlib's `ref_Vec__into_iter` for exactly the same
// reason, and prints exactly the same shape:
//
//   error [fn main]: call to 'ref_Vec__into_iter' arg 1:
//   expected &Vec<i64>, got &Vec<i64>
//
// The recorded `observed` text is unchanged, which is why the gate stayed green
// across the repair — that is the row doing its job, not a stale record.
//
// THE MECHANISM IS NOW NAMED, AND IT IS WIDER THAN THIS ROW'S ORIGINAL SITE.
// The 2026-09-10d enumeration covered the class "a decision keyed on the bare
// TYPE name `Vec`" — 11 consumers and 8 producers, all repaired. This row is a
// TWELFTH member the enumeration could not see, because it contains no `"Vec"`
// literal at all: the name is CONCATENATED into a method symbol
// (`base + "__into_iter"`). A sibling door measured the same round:
// `for x in &mut v` over a package-local `Vec` prints
// `call to 'logos.mem.collections.vec.Vec__iter_mut' arg 1: expected
// &mut Vec<i64>, got &mut Vec<i64>`. Both are the same defect as
// `bug_destructor_keyed_on_mangled_name`: an IMPL is selected by a mangled bare
// name rather than by the declaration's identity. Fixing it is a change to
// method-symbol resolution, not another call-site predicate, and that is why
// this row was NOT bought with the eleven.
//
// FIX = the diagnostic a user can act on: either the by-name impl lookup is
// keyed on the declaration's identity (leaving "no method"/"not iterable"), or
// the two types print package-qualified.
package localvec_for_ref_names_stdlib_as_slice;

pub struct Vec<T> {
    a: i64,
    b: i64,
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let v: Vec<i64> = Vec { a: 1i64, b: 2i64 };
    let mut t: i64 = 0i64;
    for x in &v {
        t = t + *x;
    }
    if t != 0i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
